;
; PION/1 -- PIO programs for the 1-bit capture and playback paths.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Timing
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The system clock is 125 MHz.  The PIO bit rate is exactly 500 kHz, i.e. one
; bit every 2 us.  Each state machine is therefore clocked at 500 kHz with
; clkdiv = 250 (125 MHz / 250 = 500 kHz), and runs ONE instruction per bit.
;
; Both the capture and the playback SM use the same divider and are enabled
; in the same cycle, so the two paths share one timebase.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Capture
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The analog front end is:
;
;     source -> preamp -> comparator ----> D flip-flop --> Q --> PIO input
;                          ^                  ^
;                          |                  |
;                       feedback         PIO side-set clock
;
; The D flip-flop is clocked by a PIO side-set pin.  The rising edge latches
; the comparator output; the PIO then samples the settled Q output.
;
; Because the flip-flop and the sample are in the same loop, the timing has
; to be explicit:
;
;   instruction 0:  side-set 1 -> CLK high  (rising edge latches D)
;   instruction 1:  side-set 0 -> CLK low
;   instruction 2:  in pins, 1               (sample Q, now settled)
;
; The sample therefore happens one PIO cycle (2 us) after the clock edge,
; which is far more than the flip-flop's t_co and than the PIO's 2-stage
; input synchroniser delay (~24 ns at 125 MHz).  The total loop latency is
; one bit period, which must be accounted for in the analog feedback path.
;
; The SM therefore runs THREE instructions per bit, so clkdiv = 125 is used
; (125 MHz / 125 = 1 MHz instruction rate, 3 instructions -> 333 kHz) -- no,
; wait.  We need the sample to happen exactly once per 2 us.
;
; Correct arithmetic: we want one SAMPLE per 2 us.  With three instructions
; per bit period we need an instruction rate of 1.5 MHz, which is not an
; integer divider of 125 MHz.  Instead we use TWO instructions per bit with
; clkdiv = 125:
;
;   instruction 0:  side-set 1 -> CLK high   (rising edge latches D)
;   instruction 1:  side-set 0 -> CLK low, and sample Q in the same cycle
;
; 125 MHz / 125 = 1 MHz instruction rate; two instructions per bit gives
; exactly 500 kHz.  The sample occurs one instruction (1 us) after the
; clock edge, which is ample for t_co and for the input synchroniser.
;
; To make the sampling instant explicit and to keep one instruction per
; bit *for the sample itself*, the sample is taken on the second
; instruction of the pair:
;

.program pion1_capture
.side_set 1
.wrap_target
    set x, 0 side 1     ; CLK high: rising edge latches the comparator into D
    in pins, 1 side 0   ; CLK low, and sample the settled Q output

;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Playback
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The playback SM pushes 1 bit per cycle onto the output pin.  Autopull
; refills the output shift register every 32 bits, so the DMA can feed it a
; whole word at a time.
;
; The shift direction is RIGHT, matching the capture direction: bit 0 of the
; word is the earliest sample.  That means a word captured from the input
; can be replayed on the output with no bit reversal anywhere.
;

.program pion1_playback
.wrap_target
    out pins, 1

;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Modulator clock
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; A free-running square wave used to clock the analog modulator in
; experiments where the modulator needs a symmetric drive.  It is not used
; by the default capture path (the capture SM provides its own clock) but is
; available for the modulator experiments described in docs/HARDWARE.md.
;

.program pion1_modclock
.side_set 1
.wrap_target
    set x, 0 side 1
    set x, 0 side 0
